Yes, that's what a make error looks like.  The patch is broken, or
didn't apply correctly.  Welcome to the world of Nethack patching,
bleh.

It's possible that the version of the patch you're applying is for an
older version of Slash.  Unlike vanilla Nethack, whose releases move
at a glacial pace, Slash is more of a moving target, and one has to
pay more attention to the version a patch is intended for.  Or (and
this is pretty likely in fact) the problem might relate to line
endings in the source.  DOS tools often expect a line to end with a
carriage return and line feed character (CR+LF), while Unix and Linux
like just LF.  If patch expects one and finds the other, that can
produce errors or incomplete patching.

Which should you use?  Do you need to convert either the patch and/or
the whole source tree?  Honestly, I don't know off-hand.  It's been a
while since I played around with patches, and I'm having to relearn
some things myself....

